To rebuild everything from scratch, run

make clean
make

make creates all data files and plots

The tasks can also be run individually as

./main A1
./main A2
./main B
./main C

The plot targets can be run individually as

make plot
make plotB
make plotC

main.cc: Contains the task functions run_task_A1(), 
run_task_A2(), run_task_B(), and run_task_C(). The 
command-line argument chooses which task to run.

neural.h: Declares the ann struct. The network stores the 
activation function, its first derivative, its second 
derivative, its antiderivative, and the network parameter 
vector p.

neural.cc: Implements the ann constructor, response(x), 
train(x,y), derivative(x), second_derivative(x), and 
antiderivative(x).

minimization.h / minimization.cc: Contains the Newton 
minimization routines used for training. There is a 
numerical-gradient version, a central-difference version, 
and an overload that accepts an analytic gradient. 
The analytic-gradient overload is used for interpolation 
training in tasks A and B.

linalg.h: Contains the pp::vector, pp::matrix, and QR 
decomposition code used by the minimizer.

Makefile: Builds the executable and regenerates plots 
and data files.

Generated files:

A1_txt contains the output from the manual response test.

A2_txt contains the printed minimizer information from training the network
for task A2.

B_txt contains the printed minimizer information from task B.

C_txt contains the printed minimizer information from task C, including the
final cost and the checks of the boundary conditions F(0) and F'(0).

network.data contains x, the exact function g(x), and the trained network
response F_p(x). training.data contains the training points used in task A2.

derivatives.data contains the data for task B: the exact function, the network
approximation, the first derivative, the second derivative, and the
antiderivative.

ode_network.data contains the data for task C: the exact ODE solution, the
network approximation, and the residual F''(x)+F(x).

network.png is the plot for task A, derivatives.png is the plot for task B,
and ode_network.png is the plot for task C.

./main A1
Runs a manual test of the neural-network response function.
It creates a network, assigns parameters to three neurons by hand,
and prints x and F_p(x) for x from -1 to 1. No training is done in A1.


The minimizer uses a small Hessian regularization and a line search.
If the Newton direction is not a descent direction, the code falls back to
steepest descent. This makes the neural-network training more stable,
especially for task C.


AI was used while developing, debugging, and explaining the code.


Summary of the explanations discussed with ai:
The network is a three-layer feedforward network with one input, several
hidden neurons, and one output. The output is

    F_p(x) = sum_i w_i f((x - a_i)/b_i)

where f is the activation function. For neuron i,

    a_i = shift / center
    b_i = scale / width
    w_i = weight

The parameter vector p stores the parameters neuron by neuron:

    p[3*i]     = a_i
    p[3*i + 1] = b_i
    p[3*i + 2] = w_i

The activation function used in the tasks is the Gaussian wavelet

    f(z) = z exp(-z^2)

Its derivatives and antiderivative are

    f'(z)  = (1 - 2z^2) exp(-z^2)
    f''(z) = (4z^3 - 6z) exp(-z^2)
    integral f(z) dz = -1/2 exp(-z^2)

These functions are passed to the ann constructor so that the same network
can evaluate F_p(x), F_p'(x), F_p''(x), and an antiderivative.



Task A:
Task A trains the network to approximate

    g(x) = cos(5x - 1) exp(-x^2)

on the interval [-1,1]. The function is sampled at 15 equally spaced
points, and the network uses 5 hidden neurons.

The cost function is

    C(p) = sum_k (F_p(x_k) - y_k)^2

The factor 1/N from the lecture notes is omitted because multiplying the
cost by a constant does not change the position of the minimum.

The initial neuron centers a_i are distributed evenly over the data
interval. The scales b_i are initialized to the spacing between the
centers. The weights w_i are initialized to 1.

The reason b_i cannot be initialized to 0 is that b_i appears in the
denominator of (x-a_i)/b_i. The reason w_i is initialized to 1 is not that
w_i = 0 is forbidden. It is because w_i multiplies the whole neuron
response. If w_i is initially zero, that neuron is switched off, and
changing a_i or b_i initially has no effect on that neuron's contribution.

The training uses an analytic gradient of the cost function. For

    z_i = (x - a_i)/b_i

the needed derivatives are

    dF/dw_i = f(z_i)
    dF/da_i = -(w_i/b_i) f'(z_i)
    dF/db_i = -(w_i z_i/b_i) f'(z_i)

The analytic gradient is used because numerical gradients were not accurate
enough for this problem. The Hessian in the minimizer is still estimated
numerically from the gradient.

Task A1:
    A manual test of response(x). The first three neurons are assigned
    hand-picked parameters and the network response is printed.

Task A2:
    Trains the network and writes network.data and training.data. The plot
    network.png compares the exact function, the trained network, and the
    training points.


Task B: 
After training, the network can also return analytic derivatives. From

    F_p(x) = sum_i w_i f(z_i),     z_i = (x-a_i)/b_i

the chain rule gives

    F_p'(x)  = sum_i (w_i/b_i) f'(z_i)
    F_p''(x) = sum_i (w_i/b_i^2) f''(z_i)

If A_f'(z) = f(z), then an antiderivative of the network is

    A_p(x) = sum_i w_i b_i A_f(z_i)

The antiderivative is only defined up to an arbitrary constant. In the plot
I subtract the value at x=-1, so the plotted antiderivative starts at zero.

Task B writes derivatives.data and make plotB creates derivatives.png.
The plot compares the trained network to the exact function and compares
the first and second derivatives to the exact derivatives. The second
derivative is less accurate than the function itself because differentiation
amplifies fitting errors.


Task C:
Task C uses the network to approximate a solution of

    y'' + y = 0

on

    [0, 2*pi]

with boundary/initial conditions

    y(0) = 0
    y'(0) = 1

The exact solution is

    y(x) = sin(x)

For this equation the differential-equation residual is

    R_p(x) = F_p''(x) + F_p(x)

The cost function is

    C(p) = integral_a^b R_p(x)^2 dx
           + alpha (F_p(c) - y_c)^2
           + beta  (F_p'(c) - y'_c)^2

In the code,

    a = 0
    b = 2*pi
    c = 0
    y_c = 0
    y'_c = 1
    alpha = 100
    beta = 100

The integral is approximated with the trapezoidal rule using 40
subintervals. Task C uses the central-difference Newton minimizer.

The minimizer reaches the maximum number of iterations in the current run,
so it prints

    Converged = false

This does not mean the result is unusable. It only means that the gradient
stopping criterion was not reached before max_steps. The boundary conditions
are still satisfied accurately, and the plot shows that the network follows
sin(x) well. The remaining error is shown in the residual plot
F_p''(x)+F_p(x).

Task C writes ode_network.data and make plotC creates ode_network.png.


Comments on cost
Evaluating the trained network costs O(n), where n is the number of hidden
neurons. Evaluating its first derivative, second derivative, or
antiderivative also costs O(n).

Training is much more expensive. For interpolation, one cost evaluation
uses all training points and all neurons. The analytic gradient costs
approximately O(N n), where N is the number of training points. A purely
numerical gradient with 3n parameters requires many full cost evaluations
and is therefore much more expensive.

For the ODE problem, each cost evaluation also has to evaluate the residual
at all integration points, so the cost grows with both the number of
neurons and the number of integration points.






